// http_colors.example.sleela -- specifying HTTP colors from SLeeLa source.
//
// This example shows the intended surface: a SLeeLa program names an HTTP
// color per service, in source, and the connector resolves it against the
// palette in http3_colors.conf (mirrored in http3_profile.{h,c} and
// http3_flow.py). Colors bundle wire form, flags, and the integrity profile
// (MAC / INTACTX / replay NONCE / basket) so source need not set each switch.
//
// A Wrapper (.sleela source file) declares its HTTP colors like so:

http {
    // Default color for any service that does not name its own.
    default color green;

    // Per-service colors. Each name must exist in http3_colors.conf.
    service "orders"    color red;     // binary + full integrity + basket
    service "billing"   color black;   // red, plus compressed payloads
    service "catalog"   color green;   // textual, core only (lightest)
    service "telemetry" color amber;   // textual, MAC + replay guard
}

// Equivalent imperative form (what the connector does under the hood):
//
//   http3_profile_t p;
//   http3_profile_by_color("red", &p);        // resolve the color
//   http3_profile_apply(&p, &pipe);           // configure the pipeline
//   // ... then send/verify per p.wire, p.mac_required, p.replay_guard, etc.
//
// Colors are advisory selectors over already-supported behavior; naming a
// color never changes the on-the-wire envelope layout, only which optional
// protections a connection turns on.
